ronutrient meaning that our  bodies cannot make it we have to ingest  it in our diet and the reason it's so  essential is because Celino cysteine  this amino acid right here is used in  Celino proteins  so Cellino proteins are just proteins  that have Celino cysteine in them Celina  proteins in general has a lot of very  important functions in the brain if this  is something that you're interested in  you can go check out mercury poisoning  the reason that we worry about mercury  levels and taking in you know eating too  much safe fish that has mercury in it  sometimes is because that mercury can  bind up Celino proteins and keep them  from working effectively Celino cysteine  is found in all three domains but not in  all lineages it means that there are  certain groups of organisms and these  domains that don't actually use Celino  proteins but humans are certainly ones  that do use celina proteins and thus  have to make Celino cysteine when we are  referring to Celino cysteine using the  the three-letter abbreviation for amino  acids its SEC using the one letter  abbreviation you is the one letter  abbreviation that has been designated  for Celino cysteine now we'll move on to  number 22 and remember we're talking  about number 22 of these 22 proto genic  protein the genetic amino acids so this  one right here is pyro lysine you can  see it's a little bit more complicated  we've still got our alpha carbon right  there the alpha carbon still has its  amino group it's hydrogen its carboxyl  group this right here is the R group so  you have four ch2 groups and then a  nitrogen and this probably looks  familiar to you as well this is what  lysine would look like I said lysine has  two hydrogens connected to this nitrogen  and in pyro lysine one of those  hydrogen's has been replaced with this  right here which is a pyro lean group  and so this is paralyzing it is not as  common throughout living organisms as  Selena  cystine it's most well known for being  used in some antigens methanogens being  a type of extrema file bacterium it's  not used in humans at all  it's three-letter abbreviations pyl and  it's one letter abbreviation is o so now  that we know a little bit more about  Celino cysteine and pyro lysine let's  talk about how they actually get  incorporated in translation in other  words what are their special translation  mechanisms well it turns out that they  are both coded for by stop codons so if  you have studied biology for a while you  will know that there are three different  stop codons and those are codons that  when the ribosome gets to a stop codon  during translation is thought to the  translation process typically that means  that the the protein is complete and can  be released but in these special  circumstances there can be a stop codon  that instead of being interpreted as a  stop codon is interpreted as being for a  pyro lysine or acelino cysteine instead  and so pyro lysine is encoded by you a G  and Celino cysteine is encoded by you G  a and so those are the codons that are  typically stop codons but in certain  cases which we'll go over right here  they can instead direct the cell to  incorporate a pyro lysine or acelino  cysteine instead so we'll talk about the  pyro lysine one first and they organisms  that you spiral icing like methanogens  for example have a special gene cluster  so this is a grouping of genes it's  called the the pyl or Pyle TS BCD gene  cluster these code for  special enzymes and those special  enzymes help the translation machinery  recognize you AG as requiring a pyro  lacing to be incorporated instead of  recognizing it as a stop codon salinas  cysteine is encoded even in a different  way and so encoded by that ug a codon  that would usually be a stop codon but  there is a special kind of structure  it's about sixty nucleotides long called  the Cellino cysteine insertion sequence  or Cesis in the mRNA before the the UGA  codon and so basically what that looks  like is you'v